<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tertullian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 155 – c. 220</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tertullian was a Christian writer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the first major theologian to write in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. His many works defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> against pagans and attacked heresies, and he coined much of the church's Latin vocabulary, including the term trinitas. In later life he was drawn to the rigorist Montanist movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tertullian.jpg" alt="Tertullian" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: André Thevet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tertullian2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
